    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So it really comes down to whether the disruption was something that could have been factored into the risk assessment at the start. If a tenant argues that the disruption was essentially a \"chaos factor\"—something that makes normal business operations impossible to predict or manage—does that kind of argument hold any weight, or is it considered too vague for a legal standard?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Arguments relying on a general \"chaos factor\" are usually considered too vague and are unlikely to meet the high legal standard required for force majeure relief. Courts prefer specific causation; therefore, the tenant must connect the unpredictable chaos to the lease obligation in a precise way. Instead of arguing randomness, the tenant needs strong evidence showing that the specific nature of the chaos rendered *performance* legally or physically impossible, rather than just commercially inconvenient.",
      "source": "target",
